  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 The Laws of Indices",
      "html": "<p>For any positive real $a,b$ and any real numbers $m,n$:</p><ol><li>$a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}$</li><li>$\\dfrac{a^m}{a^n} = a^{m-n}$</li><li>$(a^m)^n = a^{mn}$</li><li>$(ab)^n = a^n b^n$</li><li>$a^0 = 1$ (for $a\\ne 0$)</li><li>$a^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{a^n}$</li><li>$a^{1/n} = \\sqrt[n]{a}$ and $a^{m/n} = \\sqrt[n]{a^m}$</li></ol><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2} = 2^{4+-2} = 2^{2}$.</li><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5} = 2^{4+5} = 2^{9}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{9}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Simplify $2^5 \\cdot 2^{-2}$.",
          "a": "$2^{5+(-2)} = 2^3 = 8$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Simplify $(3^2)^3$.",
          "a": "$3^{2\\cdot 3} = 3^6 = 729$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Fractional & Negative Indices",
      "html": "<p>A fractional index means a root: $a^{m/n} = (\\sqrt[n]{a})^m$. A negative index means reciprocal: $a^{-n} = 1/a^n$.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2} = 2^{4+-2} = 2^{2}$.</li><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5} = 2^{4+5} = 2^{9}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{9}</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Evaluate (0.008) raised to the power of negative 1/3. \u2014 5.</li><li>Find the value of (1/2) raised to the power of negative 2. \u2014 4.</li><li>Evaluate 10 minus (negative 5) times 2. \u2014 20.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $27^{2/3}$.",
          "a": "$27^{2/3} = (\\sqrt[3]{27})^2 = 3^2 = 9$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $16^{-3/4}$.",
          "a": "$16^{-3/4} = \\dfrac{1}{16^{3/4}} = \\dfrac{1}{(\\sqrt[4]{16})^3} = \\dfrac{1}{2^3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Exponential Equations",
      "html": "<p>If both sides can be written with the <b>same base</b>, equate the exponents.</p>$$a^{f(x)} = a^{g(x)} \\Rightarrow f(x)=g(x)$$<h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2} = 2^{4+-2} = 2^{2}$.</li><li>Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5} = 2^{4+5} = 2^{9}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{9}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $2^{x+1} = 32$.",
          "a": "$32 = 2^5$, so $x+1=5 \\Rightarrow x = 4$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Solve $9^x = 27$.",
          "a": "$9^x = 3^{2x}$ and $27 = 3^3$, so $2x = 3 \\Rightarrow x = 3/2$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{2} \u2014 Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2} = 2^{4+-2} = 2^{2}$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 2^{9} \u2014 Add indices: $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5} = 2^{4+5} = 2^{9}$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Simplify $3^{5} \\cdot 3^{3}$.<br/><b>A:</b> 3^{8} \u2014 Add indices: $3^{5} \\cdot 3^{3} = 3^{5+3} = 3^{8}$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{-2}$.",
          "a": "2^{2}"
        },
        {
          "q": "Simplify $2^{4} \\cdot 2^{5}$.",
          "a": "2^{9}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Key definitions",
      "html": "<p>Learn these definitions exactly as stated \u2014 they are the terms examiners use.</p><ul><li><b>same base</b>: , equate the exponents</li></ul>",
      "examples": []
    }
  ],
